--mask-host for ai-jail 3.7.0: show the sandbox a host sized to its limits.
Synthetic /proc/cpuinfo (model "Virtual CPU", the host's vendor and feature
flags kept), /proc/stat, /proc/loadavg, /proc/meminfo (when a memory limit
applies) and /sys/devices/system/cpu, written at launch to a 0700 temp
directory and bound over the real ones; a live /proc/uptime (the sandbox's
own age, rewritten in place once a second, idle = age x its CPU count), so
`uptime` keeps working; --unshare-cgroup with only the sandbox's own cgroup
at /sys/fs/cgroup; DMI hidden; /etc/machine-id the sandbox's own, kept in
DIR/machine-id under --state-root. Sizes come from upstream's --cpus, the
scope's cpu.max (rounded up) and memory.max. Opt-in, Linux only, never saved:
a launch without the flag behaves as before.

Why: an agent in Zeo's jail read the host's CPU model, RAM, swap, load, uptime,
board and every sibling cgroup unit (Zeo next proposal §6.63, ask 86). It
hides, it does not isolate: cpuid, timing, uname(2), sched_getaffinity and
mount sources stay the host's. Cost: a nested sandbox cannot mount its own
/proc, which ai-jail's default Landlock and seccomp already refuse.

From the zeo-workspace/ai-jail fork, branch zeo/ebuild-370 (feat/mask-host and
its wiring to --cpus and --state-root, on upstream v3.7.0). Applies after
ai-jail-resource-limits.patch and ai-jail-state-root.patch, in that order.
Not upstream yet; it goes there as its own PR after local testing.

